  "summary": "Distinctive garden architecture from the Lingnan region of Southern China.",
  "description": "Characterized by naturalistic landscapes, integration of buildings with nature, and elaborate rockeries and water features. The style reflects the subtropical climate and the blending of Chinese garden traditions with Western influences in the modern era.",
  "culturalContext": "Lingnan gardens — Cantonese landscape architecture of Guangdong's subtropics — evolved Jiangnan garden logic for humid heat: smaller courtyards, water everywhere (pools, mist, lily ponds), open pavilions with cool draft design, Qing-style gray-brick and porcelain-work (Shiwan), and imported elements from overseas Chinese trade. Key sites: Shunde's Qinghui Garden, Panyu's Yuyin Shanfang, Dongguan's Keyuan. Lingnan's \"four famous gardens\" anchor the south's merchant-scholar identity distinct from Suzhou's reserved elegance.",
